This project is LGPLv2.1 or later

scrambla is a smb2/3 server written in python.
It is limited in scope but is complete enough to be useful for error injection
and testing clients.

The biggest missing features at this point is
* It does not fork a connection and only accests a single client connection
  at a time.
  
* is does not track uids/gids of the client and all i/o will be performed as
  root.

That said, it is complete enough to mount from a linux client,
list directories, stat files and work with files.

Add more text once the server gains more features.

Configuration
=============
Configuration is done in server/config.py.
See server/config.py.example for an example configuration file.

Authentication
==============
NTLMSSP is implemented in smb2/ntlmssp.py and SPNEGO in smb2/spnego.py, so
there is nothing to install. Clients may send their NTLMSSP tokens either
raw or wrapped in SPNEGO, both work.

You need to set up a NTLM_USER_FILE and point to it from the config with
ntlm_user_file. It holds one entry per line:

  domain:user:password

The domain matters. We advertise Config.server_name as the NTLM target
name, so a client that was not told a domain of its own will send that
name back to us and hash the password with it, and the entry has to match.
With the default server_name of SMBD that means:

  SMBD:myuser:mypassword

Getting this wrong does not look like a password problem: the client ends
up with no credentials at all and sends an anonymous authentication, which
we reject. Set guest_login to False in the config while you sort out a
login, otherwise a failed authentication quietly turns into a guest
session.

If guest_login is True, a client that fails to authenticate is let in as
guest with signing disabled. That is off when signing_required is set,
since a guest session has no session key to sign with.

Error injection
===============
Reproducers for client bugs live on their own tests/<client>_<issue>
branches, so that master stays a server that behaves. Look at what those
branches change for examples.

A command handler can return None instead of a (status, pdu) tuple, and
then no reply is sent for that command at all. That is how a reproducer
makes a client wait for a reply that never arrives, which is not
something you can do by returning an error. If every command in a
compound chain was dropped we send nothing back rather than an empty pdu.

Keying the injection off a magic file name keeps a branch to a single
change and lets the rest of the share carry on working, for example:

    if pdu['path'].decode().split('/')[-1] == 'libsmb2_issue_484':
        return None

To run it
=========
sudo PYTHONPATH=`pwd` python ./server/main.py

Directory structure
===================
smb2/ : This directory contains the mappings between bytearrays and dicts
        for the smb2/3 protocol.
	This mapping is supported for both encoding and decoding of all the
	objects and can be used to build both a client as well a server.
server/ : This directory contains the code to run a simple server.
tests/  : Test-suite to validate the marshalling/unmarshalling of the
          smb2/ code.

